# ScryRaven Cursor command hygiene

Use Windows PowerShell-native commands.

Prefer robust repo-targeted commands.

Example:

    $Repo = "C:\Users\aidan\ScryRaven"
    git -C $Repo status --short --branch

Avoid brittle command forms:
- do not rely on cd state for important Git commands;
- do not mix Bash heredocs with PowerShell;
- do not write temporary files into the repo root;
- do not use broad wildcard deletion;
- do not use destructive Git.

## Temp files

Write temporary command output, commit messages, and scratch files under $env:TEMP, not in the repository.

Allowed pattern:

    $TempPath = Join-Path $env:TEMP "scryraven_temp.txt"

Forbidden pattern:

    $TempPath = "C:\Users\aidan\ScryRaven\_commit.txt"

Only remove exact temp files under $env:TEMP.

Do not use Remove-Item against repo paths unless the user explicitly authorizes that exact deletion.

## Before committing

Before any commit, show:

    git -C $Repo status --short --branch
    git -C $Repo diff --cached --name-status
    git -C $Repo diff --cached --stat

Commit only when exactly the expected files are staged.

Do not commit untracked local artifacts, output packets, caches, logs, DB files, .env, generated private data, or unrelated files.

Use plain ASCII commit messages unless the user explicitly asks otherwise.

Use PowerShell-safe commit message handling. Prefer a temp file under $env:TEMP written with UTF-8 without BOM.

Example:

    $msgPath = Join-Path $env:TEMP "scryraven_commit_msg.txt"
    [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($msgPath, $msg, [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false))
    git -C $Repo commit -F $msgPath
    Remove-Item -Force $msgPath

Do not amend after push.

Before git commit --amend, prove all are true:
- branch has not been pushed;
- no draft PR exists yet;
- staged diff is empty unless the amend is intentionally adding staged changes;
- the user has approved the amend.

## Publication

Only push or open a draft PR when the phase prompt explicitly authorizes publication.

Safe publication shape:

    git -C $Repo push -u origin <phase-branch>
    gh pr create --draft --base main --head <phase-branch>

Never merge, squash merge, rebase, force-push, reset, clean destructively, delete branches, or mark a draft PR ready for review unless the user explicitly authorizes that exact action.

## Read-only commands

Read-only commands are generally safe:

    git -C $Repo status --short --branch
    git -C $Repo branch -vv
    git -C $Repo log --oneline --decorate -n 20
    git -C $Repo diff --stat main...HEAD
    git -C $Repo diff --name-status main...HEAD
    rg "<pattern>"
    Get-Content <repo-file>

Still avoid reading secrets, .env, DB rows, private logs, caches, raw provider payloads, raw prompts, full raw traces, local output packets, or unrelated generated artifacts.

## Tests and live calls

Offline tests are allowed when scoped by the phase.

Do not run live ScryRaven/proplex/scryraven provider, model, search, retrieval, fetch/read, or validation calls unless the phase prompt explicitly scopes live validation.

Before running broad tests, run focused tests first.

Do not loop broad tests repeatedly without a clear reason.

## Permission prompts

When asking the user to approve a command, explain:
- what the command does;
- whether it reads or writes;
- exact files or refs affected;
- why it is safe;
- what would be unsafe.

Do not ask for broad allowlisting during a ScryRaven phase. Prefer one exact command approval at a time.

## Stop conditions

Stop and ask instead of improvising if a command would require:
- destructive Git;
- branch deletion;
- history rewrite after push;
- merge or squash merge;
- force-push;
- reset or clean;
- broad filesystem deletion;
- reading secrets or generated private artifacts;
- live provider/search/model/fetch/read calls;
- changing closed surfaces outside the phase prompt.